            <div type="translation" xml:lang="en">
                <p>
                    <persName ref="#marcaridus" xml:lang="la">Flavius Marcaridus</persName>, <term xml:lang="la">tribunus militum</term> of the <term xml:lang="la">Iovii iuniores</term>, who lived 45 years.
                </p>
                <p>
                    If anyone violates this (sarcophagus), they shall pay one (Roman) pound of gold to the <term xml:lang="la">fiscus</term>.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="it">
                <p>
                    <persName ref="#marcaridus">Flavio Marcarido</persName>, <term xml:lang="la">tribunus militum</term> degli <term xml:lang="la">Iovii iuniores</term>, che visse 45 anni.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Se qualcuno violerà questa (arca), versi una libbra d'oro al <term xml:lang="la">fiscus</term>.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary" xml:lang="en">
                <p>
                    <persName ref="#marcaridus" xml:lang="la">Flavius Marcaridus</persName> was a <term xml:lang="la">tribunus</term> of the <term xml:lang="la">Iovii iuniores</term>. As Lettich notes (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#lettich1983">Lettich 1983, 90</ref>), towards the end of the 4th century <term xml:lang="la">numerorum tribuni</term> also appear in the <title xml:lang="la">Codex Theodosianus</title>, an element that would confirm a dating of the sarcophagus to that period.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The <term xml:lang="la">Iovii</term> are a clear reference to Jupiter (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#barbero2006">Barbero 2006, 70</ref>) and would form a pair with the <term xml:lang="la">Victores</term>, whose name was taken from Hercules Victor. Although Jupiter and Hercules were the protective deities of Diocletian and Maximian respectively (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#barbero2006">Barbero 2006, 70</ref>), the theory put forward by Hoffmann (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#hoffmann1969">Hoffmann 1969, 131-164</ref>), according to which these units were founded in the Tetrarchic period, has been challenged by recent scholarship (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#rocco2012">Rocco 2012, 163</ref>; <ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#barbero2006">Barbero 2006, 70</ref>).
                </p>
                <p>
                    The <term xml:lang="la">cognomen</term> <term xml:lang="la">Marcaridus</term> is of Germanic origin (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#schoenfeld">Schönfeld 1911, 161, 303-305</ref>).
                </p>
                <p>
                    Lettich (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#lettich1983">Lettich 1983, 89-90</ref>) records the sigla M(anibus) D(is) on the lid of the sarcophagus, which is now lost. However, nothing of the kind is described by Bertolini (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#bertolini1874a">Bertolini 1874a, 28</ref>; <ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#bertolini1874b">1874b, 287</ref>; <ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#bertolini1875b">1875b, 119</ref>), the first eyewitness, nor by the other critical editions.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary" xml:lang="it">
                <p>
                    <persName ref="#marcaridus">Flavio Marcarido</persName> era un <term xml:lang="la">tribunus</term> degli <term xml:lang="la">Iovii iuniores</term>. Come nota Lettich (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#lettich1983">Lettich 1983, 90</ref>), verso la fine del IV secolo compaiono anche i <term xml:lang="la">numerorum tribuni</term> nel <title xml:lang="la">Codex Theodosianus</title>, elemento che confermerebbe una datazione del sarcofago a quel periodo.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Gli <term xml:lang="la">Iovii</term> sono un chiaro riferimento a Giove (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#barbero2006">Barbero 2006, 70</ref>) e formerebbero una coppia con i <term xml:lang="la">Victores</term>, il cui nome fu ripreso da Ercole Vittore. Sebbene Giove ed Ercole fossero le divinità protettrici rispettivamente di Diocleziano e Massimiano (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#barbero2006">Barbero 2006, 70</ref>), la teoria presentata da Hoffmann (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#hoffmann1969">Hoffmann 1969, 131-164</ref>), secondo la quale tali reparti sarebbero stati fondati in età tetrarchica, è stata messa in discussione dalla storiografia recente (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#rocco2012">Rocco 2012, 163</ref>; <ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#barbero2006">Barbero 2006, 70</ref>).
                </p>
                <p>
                    Il <term xml:lang="la">cognomen</term> <term xml:lang="la">Marcaridus</term> ha origini germaniche (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#schoenfeld">Schönfeld 1911, 161, 303-305</ref>).
                </p>
                <p>
                    Lettich (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#lettich1983">Lettich 1983, 89-90</ref>) riporta le sigle M(anibus) D(is) sul coperchio del sarcofago, attualmente disperso. Tuttavia, niente del genere viene descritto da Bertolini (<ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#bertolini1874a">Bertolini 1874a, 28</ref>; <ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#bertolini1874b">1874b, 287</ref>; <ref target="../references/bibliography.xml#bertolini1875b">1875b, 119</ref>), il primo testimone autoptico, né dalle altre edizioni critiche.
                </p>
            </div>
